          "worked_solution": "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14122747/assets/2dace7c4f02fa0575d70264f33805d15470fa0894622c4d26d80fc1221aa2c54.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14122747/assets/40f0bf749448807b1a3aa2b593ae2e30acf26fabedbdc45da75e338e84c328f2.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}>0.$",
